  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening Energy Sector Resilience in Jamaica</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Jamaica):  Energy disruption is a major impediment to rapid recovery in the aftermath of a natural disaster. To bolster current efforts to enhance Jamaica&apos;s capacity to mitigate the effects of natural disasters,  USAID will support initiatives to improve energy sector resilience through the use of renewable energy and develop partnerships with small and medium enterprises (SMEs) to promote the adoption of distributed renewable energy, enhance resilience, and reduce grid disruptions. Illustrative activities include: 1) development of outreach materials focused on SMEs; 2) coordination with business associations to identify members who are interested in distributed renewable energy and conduct outreach; 3) identification of businesses in these sector(s) interested in acquiring solar and battery systems and facilitating deals with investor partners; and 4) engagement of the financial sector on the perceived risks and opportunities of lending for solar systems and financial products available to support SME adoption...While many emergency facilities have back-up diesel generators, their length of service depends upon fuel storage and resupply, where solar systems with batteries can operate indefinitely, making them resilient under the harsh weather conditions that have been seen in some Caribbean countries in recent years. In partnership with the GoJ and the private sector, USAID will identify and plan a new solar and battery system for a critical emergency facility based on the potential for distributed solar energy systems with batteries to reduce electricity costs by up to 30 percent, thereby allowing an emergency facility to be completely or partially grid-independent...USAID will document lessons learned on the development of distributed renewable energy for dissemination and replicability in other Caribbean countries..</narrative>
  </description>
